    <text>
        <body>
            <div xml:id="d231">

                <pb facs="0469" n="325" sameAs="frus:frus1929v02/pg325"/>

                <note rend="inline" type="source">861.77 Chinese Eastern/390: Telegram</note>
                <head><hi rendition="#italic">The Ambassador in Germany</hi> (<hi
                        rendition="#italic">Schurman</hi>) <hi rendition="#italic">to the Secretary
                        of State</hi></head>
                <opener><dateline rendition="#float-right"><placeName><hi rendition="#small-caps"
                                >Berlin</hi></placeName>, <date calendar="gregorian"
                            when="1929-10-14T11:00:00+01:00">October 14, 1929—11
                        a.m.</date><lb/>[Received October 14—9:45 a.m.]</dateline></opener>
                <p>194. During a visit of Wiley<anchor n="93" xml:id="d231-f1"/> to Chief of
                    American Division of Foreign Office on Saturday,<anchor n="94" xml:id="d231-f2"
                    /> De Haas suggested that Wiley see Trautmann, <pb facs="0470" n="326"
                        xml:id="pg326"/> Chief Far Eastern Division, who gave him text of note
                    telegraphed by German Government on October 7 to Chinese and Soviet Governments
                    proposing cessation of arrests of their respective nationals and reciprocal
                    release of those already arrested.<anchor n="95" xml:id="d231-f3"/> Trautmann
                    expressed the hope that the Department would adopt a friendly attitude toward
                    the project. The support of the American press too he said would be most
                    helpful.</p>
                <p>Text of note<anchor n="96" xml:id="d231-f4"/> by next pouch.</p>
                <closer><signed><hi rendition="#small-caps">Schurman</hi></signed></closer>
            </div>
        </body>
    </text>
    <standOff>
        <listAnnotation>
            <note corresp="#d231-f1" n="93" xml:id="d231-f1-content">
                <p>John C. Wiley, First Secretary of Embassy.</p>
            </note>
            <note corresp="#d231-f2" n="94" xml:id="d231-f2-content">
                <p>October 12, 1929.</p>
            </note>
            <note corresp="#d231-f3" n="95" xml:id="d231-f3-content">
                <p>The Moscow <hi rendition="#italic">Izvestia</hi> on October 18 announced the
                    Soviet Government’s refusal to accept the proposal.</p>
            </note>
            <note corresp="#d231-f4" n="96" xml:id="d231-f4-content">
                <p>Not printed.</p>
            </note>
        </listAnnotation>
    </standOff>
